### What is the Letter Case Permutation problem?

Given a string containing letters and digits, generate all possible strings that can be formed by changing the case of zero or more letters. Digits remain unchanged. For example, `"a1b"` produces `["a1b", "a1B", "A1b", "A1B"]`.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Input: "a1b"
// Letters: 'a' at index 0, 'b' at index 2
// 2^2 = 4 variants:
// "a1b", "a1B", "A1b", "A1B"
```

### Why does the doubling technique work?

This is a Subsets-pattern problem. Each letter is a binary choice: keep the original case or flip it. Digits have no choice. Processing characters left to right, the list of variants doubles at each letter and stays the same at each digit. After all characters are processed, the list contains all 2^k variants where k is the number of letters.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Input: "a1b"
// Start: ["a1b"]
// Process index 0 ('a' is letter):
//   Fork each variant: flip 'a' to 'A'
//   List: ["a1b", "A1b"]
// Process index 1 ('1' is digit):
//   No fork, list unchanged: ["a1b", "A1b"]
// Process index 2 ('b' is letter):
//   Fork each variant: flip 'b' to 'B'
//   List: ["a1b", "A1b", "a1B", "A1B"]
```

### How do you implement it?

Seed the list with the original string as a character array. For each index, check if the character is a letter. If it is, snapshot the current list size, then for each existing variant, create a copy and flip the case at that index.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class LetterCasePermutation {

    public List<String> letterCasePermutation(String s) {
        List<char[]> variants = new ArrayList<>();
        variants.add(s.toCharArray());

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (Character.isLetter(s.charAt(i))) {
                int size = variants.size(); // snapshot before forking
                for (int j = 0; j < size; j++) {
                    char[] copy = variants.get(j).clone();
                    copy[i] = flipCase(copy[i]);
                    variants.add(copy);
                }
            }
        }

        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
        for (char[] v : variants) result.add(new String(v));
        return result;
    }

    private char flipCase(char c) {
        if (Character.isUpperCase(c)) return Character.toLowerCase(c);
        return Character.toUpperCase(c);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LetterCasePermutation solver = new LetterCasePermutation();
        System.out.println(solver.letterCasePermutation("a1b"));
        System.out.println(solver.letterCasePermutation("3z4"));
    }
}
```

```text
[a1b, A1b, a1B, A1B]
[3z4, 3Z4]
```

### Why must each fork be an independent copy?

If you modify a shared character array, flipping one variant's character corrupts all variants that share the same backing array. Using `.clone()` on the character array ensures each fork is independent.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// WRONG: shared reference
char[] shared = variants.get(j); // same reference
shared[i] = flipCase(shared[i]); // corrupts the original!
variants.add(shared); // both entries point to the same array

// CORRECT: independent copy
char[] copy = variants.get(j).clone();
copy[i] = flipCase(copy[i]);
variants.add(copy); // separate array
```

### Why snapshot the list size before each letter?

Without snapshotting, the inner loop would iterate over variants added during the same round, causing each new variant to be forked again and producing duplicates or incorrect results. Snapshotting the size ensures you only fork variants that existed before this letter's processing.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// WRONG: no snapshot
for (int j = 0; j < variants.size(); j++) { // size grows during loop!
    char[] copy = variants.get(j).clone();
    copy[i] = flipCase(copy[i]);
    variants.add(copy); // infinite loop!
}

// CORRECT: snapshot size
int size = variants.size();
for (int j = 0; j < size; j++) {
    char[] copy = variants.get(j).clone();
    copy[i] = flipCase(copy[i]);
    variants.add(copy);
}
```

### How does the backtracking alternative work?

Instead of the iterative doubling approach, you can use recursion. At each index, if the character is a digit, recurse to the next index. If it is a letter, recurse twice: once with the original case and once with the flipped case.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class LetterCasePermutationRecursive {

    public List<String> letterCasePermutation(String s) {
        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
        backtrack(s.toCharArray(), 0, result);
        return result;
    }

    private void backtrack(char[] chars, int index, List<String> result) {
        if (index == chars.length) {
            result.add(new String(chars));
            return;
        }
        if (Character.isDigit(chars[index])) {
            backtrack(chars, index + 1, result);
        } else {
            // Keep original case
            backtrack(chars, index + 1, result);
            // Flip case
            chars[index] = flipCase(chars[index]);
            backtrack(chars, index + 1, result);
            // Restore original (backtrack)
            chars[index] = flipCase(chars[index]);
        }
    }

    private char flipCase(char c) {
        if (Character.isUpperCase(c)) return Character.toLowerCase(c);
        return Character.toUpperCase(c);
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include: all digits (only one variant, the original), all letters (2^n variants), empty string (one empty variant), and single character.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Edge case 1: "123" -> ["123"] (no letters, one variant)
// Edge case 2: "abc" -> 2^3 = 8 variants
// Edge case 3: "" -> [""] (one empty variant)
// Edge case 4: "A" -> ["A", "a"] (two variants)
// Edge case 5: "a1B2c" -> 2^3 = 8 variants (3 letters)
```

### What about a banking use case?

At the National Bank of India, support needs to match customer-typed reference codes against stored records. Customers may type codes in any case, so the system generates all case variants while keeping digits fixed.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.HashSet;

public class ReferenceCodeMatcher {

    private final LetterCasePermutation solver = new LetterCasePermutation();

    public boolean matchesAnyVariant(String typedCode, Set<String> storedCodes) {
        List<String> variants = solver.letterCasePermutation(typedCode);
        for (String variant : variants) {
            if (storedCodes.contains(variant)) {
                System.out.println("Matched variant: " + variant);
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ReferenceCodeMatcher matcher = new ReferenceCodeMatcher();
        Set<String> stored = new HashSet<>();
        stored.add("NBI2024x");

        matcher.matchesAnyVariant("nbi2024X", stored);
    }
}
```

```text
Matched variant: NBI2024x
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should note that only alphabetic characters fork the result, so a code with many digits and few letters produces a small result even for long strings. The iterative doubling approach avoids recursion overhead and is often preferred in interviews for its clarity. Using a bit mask over the letter positions is an alternative: each bitmask from 0 to 2^k - 1 determines which letters to flip, generating all variants without explicit list management.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Senior notes:
// 1. Result size: 2^k where k = number of letters
// 2. Many digits + few letters -> small result even for long strings
// 3. Bitmask alternative: iterate 0 to 2^k - 1, flip letter at bit i
// 4. Iterative doubling avoids recursion stack overhead
// 5. Character.isLetter, toLowerCase, toUpperCase handle Unicode correctly
```
